### Model answer

1. (a) The ELF header's `Type` field: `EXEC` (the fixed-address executable, mapped at its recorded file addresses) versus `DYN` (the position-independent object the loader maps like a shared library), plus the dynamic section's `(FLAGS_1) PIE` tag on the PIE executable. Nothing in the IR marks the decision — the two modules are byte-identical.

2. (b) `R_X86_64_RELATIVE`: the loader writes `load_base + offset` (the records read `*ABS*+0x16e0` and so on) into the executable's own writable slots — here the `.fini_array` entry at `0x27c0`, the `.init_array` entry at `0x27c8`, and a `.data` slot at `0x39b0`; the `.got` is not among them, its library slots still coming from the `GLOB_DAT` records both builds carry. The self-relocations exist only in the PIE executable, because its own base — and therefore its own addresses — is randomized at load time.

3. (c) `.got.plt`, the jump table lazy binding fills: in the default build it sits at `0x39c0` next to `.data`, outside the RELRO segment, so it stays writable. The full-RELRO linker moves it to `0x29b8`, inside the widened `PT_GNU_RELRO` segment (whose end is exactly `.got.plt`'s end), and the dynamic section gains `BIND_NOW`, so every jump slot is resolved eagerly at load. After load, the whole RELRO range — both GOT tables — is read-only, and a write to a jump slot faults.
